course: dictionary
lesson: 3639
title: Proper nouns: names, people and places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A proper noun is the name of one particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter. Finn is a proper noun. «Finn's late mornings went another way» means one named boy, not any boy. Common nouns like boy or country do not need capitals unless they start a sentence.
explain: The same word can be a name or a word for people from a place. Finn with a capital is a name for one person, while the Finns means the people of Finland. Check the sentence to decide which one it is.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Rory missed football practice as a punishment, and kept looking at Finn.» Finn is a person here, because Rory is looking at him. Ask yourself: is this a person, a place or a thing? Then decide whether it is a name.
fact: A proper noun names one particular person, place or thing, and starts with a capital letter.
fact: Finn is a boy's first name, popular in Ireland, the UK, Australia, New Zealand and the USA.
fact: A Finn is a person from Finland, a country in northern Europe, and the Finns are its people.
fact: Finnish is the language spoken in Finland, and it starts with a capital letter.
fact: Finn McCool, also called Fionn mac Cumhaill, is a giant hero of Irish legends, and Irish tales say he built the Giant's Causeway.
exercise: Which word is a proper noun in «Finn's late mornings went another way.»? | went | late | *Finn's | mornings
exercise: What is a Finn? | A word for a loud puddle | *A person from Finland, a country in northern Europe | A giant hero of Irish legends | A type of football practice
exercise: Which sentence uses capital letters correctly? | Finnish is the language of finland | *Finnish is the language of Finland | finnish is the language of finland | finnish is the language of Finland
test: Who is Finn McCool in the old Irish stories? | A town in Finland | A kind of puddle | *A giant hero who built the Giant's Causeway | A boy in an American novel
test: In «Finn told his mum, knocked on the man's door with her, said sorry and helped pay for the glass», what does Finn do? | He blames his best friend | He runs away from the mess | He ignores the broken glass | *He owns up and tries to make things right
exam: Which of these is a proper noun? | *Finland | happily | puddle | gate
